When our client asked for a full visual refresh with a one-day turnaround, the honest answer should have been no. Instead we said yes, because six months earlier we'd quietly finished moving every color, spacing value, and font-size in the product to CSS custom properties.

## What actually took a day

Rewriting a design isn't the same as rewriting layout. We weren't moving boxes around — we were changing what color the boxes were, how much air sat between them, and which typeface carried the headlines. All three of those had already been reduced to a single source of truth:

```css
:root {
  --color-ink: #121212;
  --color-paper: #ffffff;
  --color-accent: #2a3d8f;
  --space-4: 16px;
  --font-display: 'Source Serif 4', serif;
}
```

Swap the values, ship the build, done. No component needed to know that its border color had changed, because no component ever hardcoded a border color.

## What we hadn't tokenized

Icon stroke widths. Shadow blur radii. The exact easing curve on hover transitions. None of these were load-bearing for the redesign we got asked for, but each one was a reminder that "design tokens" isn't a checkbox — it's an ongoing inventory of every visual decision you're willing to let someone change without touching a component file.

## The actual lesson

A token system doesn't save you time on the day of the redesign. It saves you the six months before it, spent being disciplined about a `--space-3` instead of a stray `14px` in a stylesheet. The redesign was fast because the discipline was already boring.
